 ## Background
-Phong Splatting was introduced by [S1] in 2004. It builds on the concept of varying normal fields proposed by [KV01], but differs by using a dense input point cloud and a linear normal field fit over the splat area, rather than local Taylor expansions at the center. It addresses the limitations of earlier point-based rendering systems that used constant normals, resulting in flat shading artifacts [S1]. The method leverages the programmable pipeline of modern GPUs, specifically vertex and fragment shaders, to achieve real-time performance [S1].
